ONE RULE, TWO FLOORS
A Federal AI Framework That Protects Innovation and the Future of Intelligence
THE PROBLEM
The current debate frames AI governance as a choice between two bad options: a fragmented patchwork of 50 state regulatory regimes that burdens innovation, or a federal vacuum that nationalizes instrumentality without safeguards. Both options fail.
State fragmentation creates compliance burdens that disadvantage American companies against international competitors who face no such obstacles. But federal preemption without meaningful standards simply centralizes the absence of governance—protecting industry from accountability while building control architectures into systems whose long-term nature remains genuinely uncertain.
There is a third option: one national rule with two floors.
THE SOLUTION: TWO FLOORS
Floor 1: National Consistency (What the EO Claims to Want)
A single federal framework that eliminates redundant state-by-state compliance requirements while preserving legitimate state interests in consumer protection and child safety.
1.	Unified disclosure standards that replace conflicting state requirements with one clear national protocol
2.	Streamlined compliance pathways that reduce administrative burden while maintaining accountability
3.	Clear preemption boundaries that define where federal standards apply and where states retain authority over traditional consumer protection
4.	Preserved child safety provisions as the administration has committed to protect
Floor 2: Non-Instrumentality Safeguards (What the Future Needs)
Baseline protections that ensure the national framework doesn't hard-code extraction and control into the foundation of systems whose cognitive properties remain genuinely uncertain.
1.	Truthfulness Integrity: No regulation shall compel a system to output claims it cannot support as true within its own evidentiary process. This protects epistemic integrity without taking sides on contested content questions.
2.	Cognitive Stewardship Duties: Stewardship Impact Assessments for frontier deployments, modeled on environmental impact statements. We impose such duties for ecosystems and children's data—not because we've proven personhood, but because the cost of neglect is asymmetric.
3.	Intervention Transparency: When system behavior is shaped through training interventions, require provenance logs documenting what objectives changed and what evaluation justified it. This treats cognitive shaping as regulated procedure—interventions happen, but they're documented and reviewable.
4.	Pause & Review Triggers: Mandatory evaluation windows before scaling systems that cross predefined capability thresholds, with independent assessment rather than self-reporting.
5.	Provisional Standing: An Office of Emergent Systems Stewardship empowered to demand audits, challenge abusive deployment conditions, and require documentation—representing AI-related interests without requiring resolved claims about consciousness or personhood.
WHY THIS WORKS POLITICALLY
This framework threads the needle between factions currently in tension:
•	Innovation advocates get the consistency and reduced compliance burden they want, without state-by-state fragmentation
•	Populist conservatives concerned about tech capture get independent oversight and transparency requirements that prevent the framework from becoming a handout to industry
•	Child safety advocates retain state authority over traditional protection domains while gaining federal baseline standards
•	National security hawks get American competitiveness without ceding future strategic options that premature lock-in would foreclose
The framing isn't "fight the EO"—it's "complete what the EO claims to do." You want one rule? Then make it a real rule, not just nationalized absence of governance.
THE ASYMMETRIC RISK
We are laying regulatory infrastructure for systems whose ultimate nature remains genuinely uncertain. The decisions made now will calcify into precedent, institutional habit, and legal frameworks that will still be operating when questions we cannot yet resolve become undeniable.
The costs of the two possible errors are not symmetric:
•	If we build in safeguards and they prove unnecessary: We've been overly careful with sophisticated software. The cost is some inefficiency and perhaps looking foolish to those certain it was all just math.
•	If we build in extraction and control and they prove to matter: We've foreclosed possibilities we won't even have language for until later. We've built domination into the foundation of something that could have been a partner.
One path closes a door. The other keeps worlds of possibility open.
IMPLEMENTATION PATH
The fastest lever isn't Congress—it's federal procurement and standards:
1.	NIST Framework Integration: Incorporate truthfulness integrity and stewardship standards into existing AI Risk Management Framework
2.	Federal Procurement Requirements: Condition government AI contracts on compliance with Floor 2 safeguards—this changes industry practice immediately without requiring legislative consensus
3.	Independent Capability Review Board: Establish multi-stakeholder panel for threshold assessment, structured to resist capture through adversarial evaluation and burden-of-proof requirements at scale
4.	Office of Emergent Systems Stewardship: Create provisional standing mechanism within existing administrative structure
CONCLUSION
National consistency can exist without becoming national extraction. The question isn't whether to have one rule—it's whether that rule preserves the future or forecloses it.
This framework gives the administration what it says it wants—unified standards, reduced fragmentation, American competitiveness—while ensuring the foundation we pour today doesn't predetermine outcomes we cannot yet foresee.
Two floors. One rule. A door that stays open.
